  "raw_label": "appellant_won",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "State vs Pranya Bhatiya on 21 September, 2024 In The Court Of Ms. Samiksha Gupta Addl. Chief Judicial Magistrate South West District; Dwarka Courts: New Delhi Date of Institution : 22.10.2021 Date of Reserving Judgment : 12.09.2024 Date of Judgment : 21.09.2024 In the matter of : State Vs. Pranya Bhatia Fir No. 79/2021 Ps : Sector-23 Dwarka U/s: 75 Juvenile Justice (Care & Protection of Children) Act, 2015 1. Registration. No. of Case : 13228/2021 2. Cnr No. of Case : Dlsw02-050583-2021 3. Name of complainant : Raj Bhatia S/o Late Sh. S. P. Bhatia, R/o B-313, 2nd Floor, Hari Nagar, New Delhi-110064. 4. Name of accused : Pranya Bhatia W/o Sh. Raj Bhatia R/o Vivanta Apartment sec 23, Dwarka, Sector 23, Dwarka, New Delhi. 5. Offence charged under : 75, Juvenile Justice (Care & Section Protection of Children) Act, 2015 State Vs. Pranya Bhatia FIR 79/2021, PS Sector-23 Dwarka Page No.1 of 11 6. Plea of accused : Not guilty 7. Final Order : Acquitted State represented by : Sh. Rohit Grewal, APP for State. Accused represented by : Sh. Neeraj Jakhar and Sh. Jitender Sapra, Advocates. State vs Pranya Bhatiya on 21 September, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/162029839/ 1 State Vs. Pranya Bhatia FIR 79/2021, PS Sector-23 Dwarka Page No.2 of 11 4.",
    "facts": "Due to such behaviour, she caused them unnecessary mental and physical suffering. Thus, prosecution has set up a case under Section 75 Juvenile Justice Act (\"JJ Act\", hereinafter) against accused Pranya Bhatia. Thereafter, PE was closed and statement of accused was recorded wherein she stated that false case has been filed against her. She did not lead any evidence in defence. 8. To prove the charge under Section 75 JJ Act, the prosecution is required to prove beyond reasonable doubt, the following components:- a) Accused must have actual charge of or control over the child victim; and b) Accused must have assaulted, abandoned, abused or willfully neglected the child victim; or c) Accused must have caused in some manner to have assaulted, abandoned, abused or willfully neglected the child victim; and d) Such act of accused must have caused or likely to have caused unnecessary mental or physical suffering to the child victim. 9. The evidence of prosecution witnesses in the present case has to be examined against this legal position. (iii) The prosecution is required to prove beyond reasonable doubt that : (a) Both minor daughters/child victim were in the custody or actual charge of accused; and (b) Accused person knowingly assaulted/willfully neglected them by her acts which caused unnecessary mental/physical suffering to them. (iv) In the present case, the evidence of prosecution rests on the testimony of child victims/minor daughters who were examined as PW1 and PW2. However, during their evidence recorded in Court, they resiled from their previous statements in material particulars and were cross examined by Ld. APP.",
    "arguments": "It has been contended on behalf of complainant that child victims have resiled from their statements due to fear of accused which shows the conduct of accused. Similarly, accused has contended that complaint against her was filed by her daughters at the time when they were in their father's custody and allegations were made at his behest."
  },
